WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.003
Rewind helps you fix damaged database values without losing newer

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:08.006
work. Here, a bad import multiplied one hundred twenty invoice

00:00:08.006 --> 00:00:11.208
prices by one hundred. These invoices are fictional.

00:00:11.858 --> 00:00:15.418
I choose only the damaged prices. Rewind shows each current

00:00:15.418 --> 00:00:18.266
price beside its value in the good backup.

00:00:18.916 --> 00:00:22.886
Restoring the whole backup would lose seven new invoices and

00:00:22.886 --> 00:00:26.856
reset twenty payment updates. This plan keeps those changes and

00:00:26.856 --> 00:00:28.444
restores only the prices.

00:00:29.094 --> 00:00:32.934
Vultr AI writes the recovery program. It runs on copies

00:00:32.934 --> 00:00:36.774
in an isolated container. A separate checker reads the result

00:00:36.774 --> 00:00:38.310
and compares every value.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.040
The one hundred twenty prices are fixed. All one hundred

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.120
twenty seven invoices remain, including seven newer invoices and
twenty

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:47.936
payment updates.

00:00:48.586 --> 00:00:52.762
All checks passed. Five isolated runs finished, and every
temporary

00:00:52.762 --> 00:00:56.938
container was removed. The checked database is ready to download.

00:00:57.588 --> 00:01:01.555
I download the recovery copy and its checks. Rewind restores

00:01:01.555 --> 00:01:04.332
the damaged prices and keeps today's work.

00:01:05.037 --> 00:01:08.539
Here is a separate containment test. This fixed program tries

00:01:08.539 --> 00:01:12.041
to run forever. The Vultr sandbox stops it at the

00:01:12.041 --> 00:01:14.493
two second limit and removes the container.
